            "pages": {
              "end": 5,
              "start": 5
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The dispute centers on whether statements obtained after an indictment are protected by the Sixth Amendment right to counsel and whether such statements, and any evidence derived from them, must be excluded."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "ad9797ec722593b68c1ac8140c40d9cdec4bff17e406a7bb3a7151b6990aece9",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-ad9797ec7225-0000-0002"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 5,
              "start": 5
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Justice Kennedy asked whether police officers generally know that once an indictment is filed, the Sixth Amendment applies in addition to the Fifth Amendment."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "ad9797ec722593b68c1ac8140c40d9cdec4bff17e406a7bb3a7151b6990aece9",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-ad9797ec7225-0000-0003"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 10,
              "start": 10
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Justice Scalia asked whether the issue of deliberate elicitation is a legal conclusion."
      }
    ],
